Good, `ref.mounted` exists.

Now let's think about whether this refactor is worth the risk. The alternative is to keep the StreamProviders and implement pagination by accumulating in the widget. But the current tests only cover first page. The user wants 100% functional, and the summary explicitly says pagination is a Phase 0 item. Let's do it.

Detailed design in `data_providers.dart`:

```dart
/// One page returned by a paginated service call.
class PageResult<T> {
  const PageResult({required this.items, required this.total});
  final List<T> items;
  final int total;
}

/// Shared accumulation logic for paginated lists.
abstract class PaginatedNotifier<T> extends AsyncNotifier<PaginatedList<T>> {
  int get pageSize => 20;
  Object keyOf(T item);
  Future<PageResult<T>> fetchPage({required int offset, required int limit});

  int _generation = 0;
  bool _disposed = false;

  @override
  Future<PaginatedList<T>> build() async {
    _disposed = false;
    final sub = DataSync.instance.changes.listen((_) => _reload());
    ref.onDispose(() {
      _disposed = true;
      sub.cancel();
    });
    final page = await fetchPage(offset: 0, limit: pageSize);
    return PaginatedList<T>(
      items: page.items,
      page: 1,
      pageSize: pageSize,
      hasMore: page.items.length < page.total,
    );
  }

  Future<void> loadMore() async {
    final current = state.valueOrNull;
    if (current == null || current.isLoading || current.isLoadingMore || !current.hasMore) {
      return;
    }
    final generation = _generation;
    state = AsyncData(current.copyWith(isLoadingMore: true));
    try {
      final page = await fetchPage(offset: current.items.length, limit: pageSize);
      if (_disposed || generation != _generation) return;
      final seen = current.items.map(keyOf).toSet();
      final merged = [...current.items, ...page.items.where((i) => !seen.contains(keyOf(i)))];
      state = AsyncData(PaginatedList<T>(
        items: merged,
        page: current.page + 1,
        pageSize: pageSize,
        hasMore: merged.length < page.total,
      ));
    } catch (_) {
      if (_disposed || generation != _generation) return;
      state = AsyncData(current);
    }
  }

  Future<void> refresh() async {
    final generation = ++_generation;
    try {
      final page = await fetchPage(offset: 0, limit: pageSize);
      if (_disposed || generation != _generation) return;
      state = AsyncData(PaginatedList<T>(
        items: page.items,
        page: 1,
        pageSize: pageSize,
        hasMore: page.items.length < page.total,
      ));
    } catch (e, st) {
      if (_disposed || generation != _generation) return;
      state = AsyncError(e, st);
    }
  }

  Future<void> _reload() async {
    // A data-sync ping means rows changed; reload the first page. Keep the
    // current items visible while the request is in flight.
    final current = state.valueOrNull;
    final generation = ++_generation;
    try {
      final page = await fetchPage(offset: 0, limit: pageSize);
      if (_disposed || generation != _generation) return;
      state = AsyncData(PaginatedList<T>(
        items: page.items,
        page: current?.page ?? 1,
        pageSize: pageSize,
        hasMore: page.items.length < page.total,
      ));
    } catch (_) {
      // Keep the last good page; the next ping or manual refresh retries.
    }
  }
}
```

Wait: `state = AsyncData(...)` inside `loadMore` and `_reload` while the notifier is in loading state? `loadMore` only when value exists. `_reload` may fire while loading; `state.valueOrNull` null, then fetch page 1 and set data. That could race with build's fetch; generation guard: build doesn't increment generation. A ping during build could set data before build returns, then build returns its own result and overrides. Fine. But if build's fetch is slower, the ping's result may be overwritten by build's (older) result. Minor. We could increment generation at build start and have build return the page; but state is managed by Riverpod, and setting state during build is not allowed? We can just ignore pings while `state.isLoading`. Add:
```dart
if (state.isLoading) return;
